  annotation = {中文辅助译名：可变多用途临床前 PET 系统的研究：模拟研究},
  abstract = {Preclinical positron emission tomography (PET) has been widely applied in disease and biomedical research. Most existing preclinical PET systems are designed for imaging stationary small animals such as mice and rats. There is an increasing interest in systems that can accommodate imaging of animals of different sizes and freely moving subjects. In this study, we propose a variable multi-purpose preclinical PET system that enables gantry adaptation for imaging stationary animals of various sizes. By incorporating an optical motion tracking system and motion compensation techniques, the system also supports imaging of freely moving animals, accommodating diverse scenarios. Using GEANT4 application for tomographic emission (GATE) simulation software, we performed a preliminary evaluation of the PET system with three diameters: 102.4 mm (D100-PET), 204.8 mm (D200-PET), and 409.6 mm (D400-PET). The evaluation focused on sensitivity, spatial resolution, and imaging performance for both stationary and moving phantoms, with a depth-of-interaction (DOI) resolution of 3 mm. The D100-PET system achieved better sensitivity (16.0 % within 350–650 keV energy window) and radial resolution (1.39 mm (FWHM)) at the field of view (FOV) center. It also showed improved micro Derenzo phantom imaging. Under the simulated camera noise, the phantom's position was corrected in D400-PET system. The 1.2-mm rods remained visible in the motion-compensated images. These findings demonstrate the advantages},
}

